* fix(inventory): survive transient page errors — retry, partial-data honesty, stale-serve

2026-11-12 23:45–00:30 UTC: healthy production deployments intermittently
served source:'none' / 0 units on the public inventory map (client 3965
units expected; smoke run 43335523470 pinned 5 failed checks). The anon
listings read is 4-5 sequential 1000-row PostgREST pages; ONE transient
page error aborted the entire fetch, the catch swallowed it, and the
domain fallback was status-mismatched dead code (filtered
status:'available' while every live row carries status:'active') — so the
map rendered an honest-but-empty state for full cache cycles.

Diagnosis (branch diagnose/inventory-anon): the exact wide query + all 34
columns + compounds return 200 from a cold client in ~2s; Vercel-held env
values match the GitHub secrets; no schema/policy change in the window —
the failures were transient request-level errors on individual pages.

Fix, four parts:
1. Per-page retry: each page gets one retry after a 250ms backoff, with
   the query rebuilt per attempt (supabase-js builders are single-use).
   A single blip no longer zeroes the whole map.
2. Partial-data honesty: a later page failing after retry serves the
   verified rows already fetched with complete:false instead of dropping
   everything; page 0 failing falls through to (3).
3. Stale-serve: the last fully successful payload is kept up to 10
   minutes and served (complete:false, generatedAt untouched) when a
   fresh read fails outright — a map showing verified units from seconds
   ago beats an empty map.
4. Fallback liveness: fetchDomain now accepts both 'active' (the live
   reality) and 'available' (legacy) statuses, so the second tier can
   actually return rows.

complete?: boolean added to InventoryResponse (absent = complete, for
back-compat with older producers).

Tests: __tests__/inventory-resilience.test.ts pins all four behaviors
with a stubbed @sierra-estates/db (4/4 green locally; adjacent inventory
suites 33/33 green).

* fix(inventory): type the fallback status widening at the domain boundary

* fix(inventory): clear stale-serve on cache-bust; isolate module state in publish-gate tests

bustInventoryCache() now also drops lastGoodListings — an admin bust is a
'this data changed NOW' signal, and holding the old payload as the
stale-serve fallback would re-serve exactly the rows the bust intends to
refresh.

The publish-gate suite's beforeEach now calls it: without module-state
isolation, a prior test's success bled into the next (stale-served
'supabase' rows masking the domain-tier fallback the suite pins), and the
domain-tier expectation is updated to the live status set
['available','active'] that makes the fallback actually return rows.

